<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanctuary of Hercules Victor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd–1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sanctuary of Hercules Victor at Tibur, modern Tivoli, was one of the largest religious complexes of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, built in the late 2nd and 1st centuries BCE. Raised on massive vaulted substructures over the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to the Sabine country, it combined a temple, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and porticoes around a terraced court. The cult of Hercules there was linked to the herding and trade of livestock and drew merchants and pilgrims.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanctuary-of-hercules-victor.jpg" alt="Sanctuary of Hercules Victor" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ChromosomeGun · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_Hercules_Victor_Rome_April_2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
